      "details": "Mineral in the bright highlands\nPlagioclase · 1 of 4\n0\n50\n100 weight %\n\nNo data\n\nPlagioclase is a mineral common in the Moon’s bright highland rocks; colors estimate its share of the surface material.\n\nView source ↗\nDataset details\nWhat it shows\nPlagioclase is an ingredient of rock, also found in granite on Earth. It is abundant in the Moon’s ancient highland crust.\nReading the scale\nPercentage by weight: 50% means half the modeled material by weight. Yellow means more; dark blue means less.\nHow it was made\nKaguya measured reflected sunlight in nine wavelength bands. A model compared those measurements with mixtures of minerals.\nLimits\nWeathered soil weakens the mineral signal and can make this estimate too high. Compare the Surface weathering map.\nCoverage\n50° south to 50° north. Gray grid marks the polar regions, missing measurements, or cells without a usable model fit.",
